\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The matter determinant is smallest on the identity orbit}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{The [massive precision](fdip-006D) #{Q_U} depends on the connection.
Gauge covariance makes its determinant constant on orbits. The comparison
below identifies the minimum. It is the value on the identity connection,
attained exactly when every closed walk acts as the identity on the fiber.}

\p{Let #{F} have dimension #{n} over its scalar field, and let #{Q_1} be
the scalar massive precision of the same finite graph, the same positive
edge weights, and the same mass #{m>0}. For every connection,
#{\det Q_U\ge(\det Q_1)^n}. Equality holds if and only if every closed
walk acts as the identity on #{F}. Every connection in the identity orbit
has that property. So does every connection whose holonomy acts through
the kernel of #{\rho}.}
\proof{
\p{Write #{D} for the positive diagonal operator on scalar functions with
#{D_v=m^2+\sum_{e\ni v}w_e}, and write #{D_H} for #{D} acting separately
on each fiber. The quadratic form in the connection background expands as
#{\langle f,Q_Uf\rangle=\sum_v D_v\|f_v\|^2-\sum_e 2w_e\operatorname{Re}\langle f(h(e)),U_ef(s(e))\rangle}.
The associated self-adjoint operator is #{Q_U=D_H-H_U}, where the only
nonzero blocks of #{H_U} are #{w_eU_e} and #{w_eU_e^*}. Let
#{K_U=D_H^{-1/2}H_UD_H^{-1/2}}. Then
#{Q_U=D_H^{1/2}(I-K_U)D_H^{1/2}}, so
#{\det Q_U=(\det D)^n\det(I-K_U)}. The identity connection has
#{Q_I=Q_1\otimes I_F}, hence #{(\det Q_1)^n=\det Q_I}. It is enough to
compare #{I-K_U} with #{I-K_I}.}

\p{The scalar adjacency #{A} has the same off-diagonal weights. Its
normalized operator #{K_1=D^{-1/2}AD^{-1/2}} is self-adjoint, and
#{K_I=K_1\otimes I_F}. The similar operator #{D^{-1}A} has row sums
#{\bigl(\sum_{e\ni v}w_e\bigr)/D_v<1}, because #{m>0}. The maximum
absolute row sum is strictly less than #{1}, so the spectral radius
of #{D^{-1}A} is strictly less than #{1}. Since
#{K_1=D^{1/2}(D^{-1}A)D^{-1/2}}, the operator #{K_1} has the same
spectral radius. Self-adjointness gives #{\|K_1\|=\rho(K_1)<1}.}

\p{The same comparison bounds the connection. For a section #{f}, set
#{y_v=\|f_v\|}. Each fiber of #{H_U} has operator norm #{w_e}, so
#{\|(H_Uf)_v\|\le(Ay)_v}. Conjugating by #{D^{1/2}} yields
#{\|(K_Uf)_v\|\le(K_1y)_v}. Therefore
#{\|K_Uf\|\le\|K_1y\|\le\|K_1\|\|f\|}, and #{\|K_U\|<1}. The eigenvalues
of the self-adjoint operator #{K_U} are real and lie in the open interval
#{(-1,1)}.}

\p{For every such eigenvalue,
#{\log(1-\lambda)=-\sum_{k\ge 1}\lambda^k/k}. Summing over eigenvalues
gives
#{\log\det(I-K_U)=-\sum_{k\ge 1}\frac1k\operatorname{tr}(K_U^k)}.
Expanding the trace in walks, a closed walk #{\gamma} of length #{k}
contributes its positive weight
#{w(\gamma)=\prod_{e\in\gamma}\frac{w_e}{\sqrt{D_{s(e)}D_{h(e)}}}} times the
trace of its holonomy. The holonomy is the ordered product of the fiber
maps, with the first edge on the right, because parallel transport along
#{e} sends #{f(s(e))} to #{U_ef(s(e))}. A unitary or orthogonal map has
eigenvalues on the circle, so the real part of its trace is at most #{n},
with equality exactly when the map is the identity. The trace
#{\operatorname{tr}(K_U^k)} is real and equals the sum of those real
parts against the walk weights. Hence
#{\operatorname{tr}(K_U^k)\le\operatorname{tr}(K_I^k)=n\operatorname{tr}(K_1^k)}.
Since each trace is at most the corresponding identity trace,
#{\log\det(I-K_U)\ge\log\det(I-K_I)}. The logarithm is increasing, and
#{\det(I-K_U)\ge\det(I-K_I)}. The powers of
#{\det D} cancel in the comparison. This is the asserted lower bound.}

\p{Equality of the determinants forces equality of the two series. For
every #{k\ge 1},
##{\operatorname{tr}(K_U^k)=\operatorname{tr}(K_I^k).}
Their difference is the sum, over closed walks, of the positive weight
times #{n-\operatorname{Re}\operatorname{tr}\operatorname{Hol}(\gamma)}.
Each summand is nonnegative, so each vanishes. Every closed walk
therefore acts as the identity on #{F}. Conversely, that condition makes every
walk contribution agree with the identity connection, and the
determinants agree.}

\p{These connections are exactly the identity orbit in the represented
gauge group. Choose a root in each connected component and propagate
#{\rho(U)} along any walk from that root. The product is independent of
the walk because every closed walk acts as the identity. The resulting
vertex operators #{g_v} satisfy #{U_e=g_{h(e)}g_{s(e)}^{-1}}. In the
other direction, a gauge transform of the identity has determinant
#{\det Q_I} by the covariance already cited. A holonomy in the kernel of
#{\rho} is invisible to #{Q_U}: equality sees the action on #{F}, not a
finer group element.}
}

\p{When #{F} is real, the induced action
#{W(U)=\frac12\log\det Q_U} of the [joint law](fdip-0077) is therefore
smallest exactly on this orbit. When #{F} is complex, the same bound
says that #{(\det Q_U)^{-1}} is largest there. The massless operator is
outside the argument: its identity connection can be singular, and a
nontrivial represented holonomy can leave a parallel section, so a zero
determinant does not characterize the identity orbit.}

\p{On the three-by-three torus with unit weights and mass squared equal
to #{1}, the all-positive connection lies on the identity orbit and
attains the bound. The [torus sum](fdip-0078) records that determinant.}
